            "description": "## Current MVP tracking\n\nThis is the Pig Boss release-one MVP epic. The current implementation set contains **27 Stories**, after four reviewed same-scope splits: PGB-110 and PGB-132 from PGB-78, and PGB-113 and PGB-129 from PGB-81. The published Jira contracts contain 119 prescribed acceptance cases. These splits add no product features.\n\n* [All MVP Stories](https://scubapig.atlassian.net/issues/?jql=project%20%3D%20PGB%20AND%20parent%20%3D%20PGB-66%20AND%20issuetype%20%3D%20Story%20ORDER%20BY%20key%20ASC)\n* [Work in progress](https://scubapig.atlassian.net/issues/?jql=project%20%3D%20PGB%20AND%20parent%20%3D%20PGB-66%20AND%20issuetype%20%3D%20Story%20AND%20status%20%3D%20%22In%20Progress%22%20ORDER%20BY%20key%20ASC)\n* [Completed Stories](https://scubapig.atlassian.net/issues/?jql=project%20%3D%20PGB%20AND%20parent%20%3D%20PGB-66%20AND%20issuetype%20%3D%20Story%20AND%20status%20%3D%20Done%20ORDER%20BY%20key%20ASC)\n\nUse the linked live lists for progress. Discovery and release-acceptance Tasks are separate from the 27-Story count. [PGB-114 — skill effectiveness evaluation](https://scubapig.atlassian.net/browse/PGB-114) is a separate programme, outside this MVP.\n\nCount and navigation reconciled on 15 November 2026. The original approved baseline below is retained as history. The repository delivery records are being updated in the reviewed split PRs; Jira contains the current native Story set. Delivery automations remain inactive.\n\n## Pig Boss release one — approved implementation set\n\nPig Boss is Pigboard's deterministic manager. A company deploys it once into its AWS account for multiple projects in one Jira site. Workstream identity is Jira site + immutable project ID + full label: AI-Maria-1 in two projects represents two independent streams.\n\n## Approved release scope\n\n* One canonical configuration with project-specific mappings, permitted repositories and secret references.\n* Portable scheduling policy and application logic, Jira adapter, DynamoDB scheduling state, thin Lambda wrapper and AWS deployment package.\n* One scan at a time per installation; one responsible installation per project. Multiple independent Cursor streams can run concurrently.\n* Ready to Work Story/Bug selection, queued repairs and stack updates, one occupied automated cycle per project-scoped stream and five Human Review tickets including Bugs.\n* Existing stage webhooks launch workers; separate job/Jira skills produce typed evidence. Human-invoked request-rework remains separate from story writing.\n* Customer-owned basic operating health: read-only readable/JSON inspection, completion freshness, failed/incomplete scans, budget warnings and AWS alarms with optional customer SNS routing.\n* Install disabled, preview, readiness, safe stop/reconciliation, compatible upgrade/rollback/removal; no vendor runtime access.\n\n## Original approved implementation baseline\n\n23 Stories: PGB-71 through PGB-90 plus PGB-99 (company configuration), PGB-100 (health inspection), PGB-101 (AWS health alarms).          \n104 prescribed acceptance cases; 53 product requirements; original 41 scheduling scenarios retained.          \nEstimated effort: 35.25 human working days plus installed acceptance. Each Story is one Pigboard PR, at most 20 changed files and less than two days. Estimates are planning judgments.\n\n[Full ordered plan, sizing and cases](https://github.com/danielchappell/Pigboard/blob/main/docs/delivery/manager-r1/README.md).\n[Company/health design](https://github.com/danielchappell/Pigboard/blob/main/docs/architecture/pig-boss-operations.md).\n\nThe revised design and delivery plan have two independent CLEAN reviews each. Native Story contracts were read back exactly after publication. The owner approved the complete 23-Story set on 15 November 2026 and authorized autonomous implementation, per-Story testing/review and merge after current-head CI and Codex bot approval. Execution follows the ordered delivery plan. Story status records progress; no AI execution labels are added. Delivery automations remain inactive.\n\n## Release acceptance\n\nPGB-91 verifies the installed package, including multi-project identity and health lifecycle; PGB-58 verifies actual worker delivery; PGB-92 records the later activation decision. Local checks and historical provider probes do not prove installed acceptance. A second disposable Jira project and any test notification destination need explicit access before that installed test; no resources are created by grooming.\n\n## Follow-on work\n\nPGB-94 is the high-priority path to measured 50-project capacity. Ten is a benchmark step, not a supported-project limit. Measure the workload, optimize safe reads, and only then design partitioning if needed; preserve one installation/configuration and one writer per project.\n\nPGB-95: automatic Jira/Cursor provisioning. PGB-70: worker status visibility. PGB-63 → PGB-64 → PGB-65: launch/cancel feasibility, dispatch design and grooming. PGB-62: human unblock and blocked-stage display. PGB-98: Teams blocker alerts. PGB-60: separate PR environment pipeline. PGB-96: additional QA helpers. PGB-97: other customer-cloud packages.\n\nNo worker heartbeat/transcript collection, Cursor deduplication, dispatcher, automatic recovery, SaaS or old runtime reuse in this release. Basic Pig Boss health is explicitly included.\n\n## History and evidence\n\nPGB-67/68 discovery and PGB-69 baseline grooming remain completed historical records. The isolated provider proof is PARTIAL, not release acceptance. PGB-93 records this approved company/health delta. Prior merged design/review evidence is preserved in PRs 73 and 74; this description supersedes older label-global and all-monitoring-deferred wording. Implementation and merge are now authorized for this fixed set; activation remains a separate owner gate.",
