                  "body": "# Pig Boss parallel delivery addendum — revision 1, 15 November 2026\n\nStatus: proposed reorder of the already approved fixed 23 Stories; ready for two independent Sol reviews together with the separate PGB-80 implementation plan. No requirement, dependency, acceptance, activation or merge gate is removed. This is delivery coordination, not a product redesign or a new Story.\n\nEvidence: live `/private/tmp/pgb66-live-children-20261115.json` contains all 29 Epic children, including exactly 23 Stories. Six Stories are Done (71–75 and 99); 17 remain. Current source baseline is verified merged main `9f03c1561b0e32de05cd296b24e50885f4abbe91`. Jira owns current status; this dated plan is not a second status store. Forecasts below are the approved `docs/delivery/manager-r1/plan-metadata.json` estimates, not new readiness claims. Every later Story still needs an actual current-tree implementation plan.\n\n## Two workstreams and the integration join\n\nA owns delivery skills: 76 → 77 → 78, then 79 (79 is dependency-ready now but its shared skill/manifest surfaces make this deliberate sequencing useful). B owns the manager: 80 → 81 → 82 → 83 → 84 → 85 → 86. These two chains may implement concurrently, in separate branches/worktrees. B's health 100 becomes dependency-ready after 84; 101 requires 86 and 100. After the principal chains join, integration 87 → 88 → 89 → 90 is serial. Health 100/101 can occupy the second implementation lane alongside integration 87–89 when per-Story shared-file ownership permits. This is not a claim that all 17 are independent.\n\nThe currently dependency-eligible Stories are 76, 77, 79 and 80. Start the already reviewed 76 and, only after its own plan gate, 80. Do not start four implementations just because four prerequisites are satisfied. 77's provisional plan is preserved; it does not compete with active 76 edits.\n\n| Story | Workstream / outcome | Exact prerequisite Stories | Approved forecast days / paths | Current readiness |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| PGB-76 | A: reviewer handoff | 74,75 | 1.25 / 8 | In Progress; actual reviewed plan supersedes forecast: 1.55 / 20 |\n| PGB-77 | A: prescribed QA helpers | 72,74 | 1.5 / 7 | To Do; provisional actual plan 1.75 / 18, not yet reviewed |\n| PGB-78 | A: complete QA ledger | 74,76,77 | 1.75 / 10 | Wait for 76 and 77 |\n| PGB-79 | A: human request-rework | 74 | 1.25 / 7 | Dependency-ready; sequence behind A's active work |\n| PGB-80 | B: offline complete-snapshot preview | 72,74,99 | 1.75 / 7 | Dependency-ready; concrete plan accompanies this addendum |\n| PGB-81 | B: repair-order preview | 80 | 1.5 / 7 | Wait for 80 |\n| PGB-82 | B: bounded Jira reads | 81,99 | 1.75 / 12 | Wait for 81 |\n| PGB-83 | B: disabled AWS preview installation | 82 | 1.75 / 13 | Wait for 82 |\n| PGB-84 | B: durable state inspection | 83 | 1.75 / 10 | Wait for 83 |\n| PGB-85 | B: evidence-only reconciliation | 82,84 | 1.5 / 7 | Wait for 84 |\n| PGB-86 | B: upgrade and removal | 84,85 | 1.25 / 8 | Wait for 85 |\n| PGB-100 | B/health: operating health | 83,84,99 | 1.75 / 11 | Eligible after 84; can run alongside integration when files permit |\n| PGB-101 | B/health: health alarms | 86,100 | 1.5 / 10 | Wait for both; required by final release check |\n| PGB-87 | Integration: ordinary admission | 75,76,78,82,84,85,86 | 1.75 / 10 | Explicit skills/manager join; no early apply implementation |\n| PGB-88 | Integration: queued repair admission | 79,87 | 1.25 / 5 | Requires human request skill as well as apply |\n| PGB-89 | Integration: dependent stack queue | 88 | 1.5 / 5 | Serial with 88 on apply code |\n| PGB-90 | Integration: compatible release check | 73,75,76,77,78,79,89,101 | 1.5 / 15 | Final join; PGB-58 live delivery acceptance remains a separate referenced gate |\n\n## Ownership and merge sequencing\n\n* Root maintains one active Jira queue, exact revisions, dependencies and gate evidence. Astra authors plans only. Sol implements; different Sol agents review and independently execute QA.\n* A uses `/private/tmp/pigboard-product-requirements-20261114` and owns its current skill/composer/Jira-reference/automation changes. B uses `/private/tmp/pigboard-manager-stream-20261115`, initially `codex/pgb-80-manager-preview`, and owns manager policy, preview CLI and the approved config additions. Nobody edits another agent's working tree or reverts unrelated changes.\n* Shared sources are explicit: README, ARCHITECTURE, docs/install, docs/using-pigboard, docs/workflow, config/skill-bundle, distribution/install tests, and later canonical configuration, Lambda/template and lifecycle files. A/B may each have distinct additive changes in isolated branches; neither overwrites the other's whole file. Root chooses a merge order. The second branch rebases onto verified merged main, preserves both changes, reruns affected contract/distribution tests and obtains review for any conflict resolution or new behavior. Identical reviewed file-tree preservation may be recorded as such; new changes never inherit a stale CLEAN verdict.\n* PGB-80 specifically shares README, ARCHITECTURE, manifest, install guide/installer/test with 76. Its manager/config runtime is disjoint. Final installed manifest must include both reviewer migration and preview payload, with both smoke/contract checks. Sequence publication/merge, not source ownership, through root. Do not merge either branch's historical main over the other's completed work.\n* After 84, health touches Lambda/config and later AWS template/lifecycle. Root allocates those files exclusively per active phase. Prefer 85/86 first, then health alongside 87–89; serialize any simultaneous Lambda/template/install-guide edits, or prepare them in isolated branches with explicit reviewed integration. No shared dirty checkout. If dependencies or a safe file partition are unavailable, the second slot does review/QA/preparation instead of inventing work.\n* A newly returned CI/bot/code/QA finding on a prerequisite takes priority over next-Story work in its lane and any dependent integration. Save a checkpoint, fix confirmed in-scope root causes, re-review current revision and rebind dependants after merge. Independent unaffected work in the other lane may continue. Shared contract defects pause both affected consumers. Out-of-scope defects are recorded without expanding the frozen Story.\n\n## Capacity and gates\n\nThere are four active-agent slots including root. Maximum two active implementations leaves root + one QA/review slot. A two-reviewer round needs root + two independent reviewers, so pause one implementation/planner at a saved checkpoint; pause both if an independent QA run also needs the slot. Reviewers may run sequentially if necessary, but remain two distinct independent Sol authors with complementary lenses. Do not substitute an implementer's self-review. A plan author may not independently approve its own plan.\n\nEach Story retains: actual Jira/current-source plan → two CLEAN plan reviews → implementation and meaningful tests/docs → committed exact-diff two CLEAN code reviews + independent QA → PR/current CI/current Codex completed thumbs-up → authorized merge → resulting tree verification. Plan previews are marked provisional. Local tests do not complete prescribed human acceptance or activate a customer installation. At most 20 changed source paths and less than two human working days apply independently to each complete Story, including supporting docs/tests/PR work; split before code if a genuine limit cannot be met.\n\nThe present bounded review round covers **two separately hashed artifacts**: this 17-Story reorder and PGB-80's concrete plan. Each reviewer enumerates 17/17 rows, the dependency joins/ownership/capacity/gates, and separately all PGB-80 requirements/contracts/paths, with second-sweep coverage and a verdict per artifact. Approval of the reorder is not approval of all future implementation plans.\n\nExisting QA helper defects use the owner's explicit fallback: preserve concrete failed evidence, file a post-Epic bug, stop the unsuitable helper and use direct independent QA. The missing advertised qa-database npm test file is such a pre-existing tooling defect; it does not cancel PGB-77 or authorize unbounded test infrastructure repair.\n\n## Seven-principle assessment\n\n1. Intuitive: two named delivery lanes and one explicit final join replace a misleading globally serial queue; users still see ordinary Jira Stories and outcomes.\n2. Discoverable: every remaining Story lists prerequisites, forecast and readiness. Agents receive exact working directories/file ownership and reviewers receive finite denominators.\n3. Delightful: independent work proceeds while the other lane waits for gates, without repeated approval questions or crowding out QA. Capacity limits may briefly idle one lane; this avoids costly conflicted work.\n4. Token saver: reuse ratified designs and saved provisional plans; exchange exact revisions and bounded deltas. Never conceal failed evidence or skip review to reduce calls.\n5. Secure: separate checkouts and explicit authority prevent accidental writes/reverts; provider/state/activation boundaries are unchanged. Parallel delivery grants no broader product privileges.\n6. Agents are equal: explicit machine-readable Jira dependencies and exact artifact hashes accompany human-readable queue explanations. Both lanes receive the same independent gates.\n7. Dependable: dependency-ready, planned, implemented, tested, reviewed, merged and accepted remain different states. Returned findings stop affected dependants; no stale review result or pending PR is called merged.\n\nOne canonical customer configuration remains the product authority. Parallel ownership changes implementation order only; no duplicated settings, runtime state, scheduler, monitoring, deduplication or activation is introduced.",
