            "description": "Problem to solve\n\nThe first manager must choose work deterministically, but the Epic YAML and queued-repair metadata are still drafts. Developers need one exact contract before writing the scheduler or changing worker skills.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n* Publish versioned Epic and ticket scheduling contracts with complete human-editable skeletons, visible optional nulls and precise fields required before admission.\n* Resolve the Epic opt-in label, stream membership, dependency authority, queued-repair requests, affected descendants and their ordering.\n* Specify cap accounting for repairs, cancelled or removed items, duplicate labels and human-requested unblock; preserve the accepted five-ticket cap and human-only scope decisions.\n* Map all seven storyboard scenarios to concrete input snapshots and expected selection or wait reasons, including unready Bugs, paused Epics and independent streams.\n* Define the compatible change from current Story inputs and direct rework handoffs to the new contract, including prepared Bugs and separate role-specific Jira skills.\n* Obtain two independent design reviews; return genuinely new product choices to the owner rather than inventing policy.\n\nTechnical Acceptance Criteria\n\n* Design Task in https://github.com/danielchappell/Pigboard; no runtime, live ticket transition, schema installation or automation activation.\n* Parse a Jira document code block with a normal YAML parser and versioned schema; no regex extraction, LLM interpretation or credentials in ticket data.\n* Native Jira retains status, type, parent and full numbered labels; configuration retains allowed projects, destinations and access authority.\n* Reference the first-manager design and existing scenario decisions; do not reuse the archived runtime.\n\nTesting Strategy\n\n* Manual contract validation: walk every accepted scenario and adversarial examples for missing fields, duplicate keys, unknown schema, unresolved dependencies, partial plans and conflicting stream membership.\n* Retain the example input, expected candidate or wait, field-level error and reviewed contract revision for each case.\n* Validate that every admitted ticket has an authorized bounded scope and that broader Epic context grants no additional change authority.\n* This Task produces reviewed specifications; executable QA skill mapping and full implementation acceptance cases are required in subsequent Stories.\n\n## Owner decisions — AI and human testing, 14 November 2026\n\nLaunch with the existing QA skills; new supported databases/tools are follow-on work. Every test case belongs on the ticket with stable ID, requirements, steps, expectations, prerequisites and evidence. AI-assigned cases name the exact skills and execution order. A separate human-case section names the responsible human role and is excluded from AI execution.\n\nAI QA may hand off to Human Review when its prescribed AI cases pass, reporting human cases as Pending human testing. It must not claim all ticket testing passed. An unsupported AI-assigned case still blocks for human action; agents cannot silently reassign it to a human, omit it or invent tools. A human can deliberately revise the testing contract through the normal review process.\n\nRefine the schema, QA entrypoint and handoff contract around this ownership split. Preserve complete visible skeletons and distinguish required nulls from explicitly empty case lists. Resolve the all-human case edge explicitly in the final contract.\n\n## Approved stack-repair rule — 14 November 2026\n\nThe owner approved conservative stack repair using the actual recorded parent PR/revision, not Epic order or an AI impact assessment. Each ticket retains its own PR.\n\nWhen publishing B, its implementer records B's PR, parent A's PR, parent revision incorporated and B's revision covered by verification. A confirmed repair to A uses A's existing ticket and PR. An active B finishes its existing automated cycle first; do not interrupt it or start C ahead of queued repair.\n\nAfter A's repair completes automated review and prescribed AI QA and reaches Human Review at a new revision, the manager compares B's recorded parent revision. If B still requires the recorded stack update, the manager owns Human Review -> Rework Queued, with the repair link and reason. Then normal admission moves B -> In Progress. B incorporates A's repaired code on B's branch and gets fresh review/AI QA, preserving pending human tests. Continue actual descendants oldest first; matching already-incorporated repair evidence must prevent repeated queuing.\n\nMissing/ambiguous relationships, already-merged PRs or scope growth require human intervention. Routine merge/retarget activity and an independent B do not trigger cascading. No multi-PR bug repair job, PR merge/retarget automation, interruption or additional QA skill support is authorized.\n\nThis resolves cascade policy and manager queue authority. Exact handoff schema, transition safeguards, repeat-repair behavior, five-ticket cap accounting and fixture proof remain discovery work. No live automation or ticket execution is enabled.",
