            "description": "Problem to solve\n\nA scheduled manager must advance work without creating overlapping streams or exposing customer data. AWS Lambda is selected, but the Jira read, transition and failure behavior needs evidence before apply mode can be designed.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n* Define a customer-owned Lambda deployment with no Scuba Pig role, support access, telemetry or customer-data transfer.\n* Evaluate the proposed IAM-only scheduled scan with no public endpoint, a disabled initial schedule and an operator preview that makes no writes.\n* Verify complete and sufficiently fresh Jira reads, source-status transition behavior, repeated scans, paused Epics and occupied streams.\n* Specify a bounded response to throttling, incomplete reads and an uncertain transition, including how a hold survives the next invocation and how a human reconciles it.\n* Produce the deploy/upgrade/disable/rollback/remove contract, request budget, supported pilot size, credentials, IAM/Jira permission matrix and sanitized log policy.\n* Resolve findings through independent design review; if safe admission requires extra persistent infrastructure or new policy, record that choice for the owner before implementation.\n\nTechnical Acceptance Criteria\n\n* Research/design only; AWS Lambda is fixed, while schedule cadence and deployment mechanism remain to be validated.\n* Use https://github.com/danielchappell/Pigboard; no old runtime reuse, worker run ledger, Cursor launch proxy, monitoring, duplicate-agent prevention or automatic recovery.\n* Reserved concurrency one is only overlap reduction, not a distributed lock or exactly-once guarantee.\n* Read customer secrets from their secret store; trusted configuration owns Jira origin/project/status mappings; reject ticket-selected destinations and credential-bearing redirects.\n* Document actual Jira permission breadth; do not claim runtime checks reduce credential privileges.\n* No real infrastructure provision, credential creation, live Jira write or Cursor launch is authorized by this grooming Task; arrange a separately authorized disposable fixture for live verification.\n\nTesting Strategy\n\n* Compare official AWS invocation/retry and Jira search/transition contracts with bounded fixture results where access is authorized.\n* Cover fresh selection, repeat scan after transition, search lag, two invocations, changed status before write, incomplete pages, denied access, rate limiting and lost write response.\n* Verify an unauthorized AWS principal cannot invoke apply mode; show that preview, failures and logs expose no secrets or ticket bodies.\n* Retain sanitized request/response and resource-policy evidence; distinguish documentation, simulated tests and live acceptance.\n* Stop discovery with an explicit unresolved result if missing access prevents proof; do not declare the dependent apply-mode Story ready.\n\nOwner clarification — 14 November 2026: evaluate the AWS installation as a company administrator journey, including a small configuration file, an inspectable infrastructure-as-code change, use of existing customer secret/artifact resources where appropriate, disabled first deployment, clear outputs, update and removal. Choose and justify one supported packaging route for release one; do not promise every IaC framework. Freeze the boundary between cloud-independent manager logic/application orchestration, the Jira adapter, thin Lambda invocation wrapper, and AWS infrastructure package. No AWS-specific types, SDK imports, resource identifiers or environment lookups may leak into manager rules. Verify the same logic runs locally without AWS credentials. Other-cloud wrappers remain follow-on work.\n\n## Approved state decision — 14 November 2026\n\nThe owner selected DynamoDB for the first customer-owned AWS package. This supersedes the earlier no-database wording only for admission state and its safe lifecycle. S3 is not the operational-state store. No worker run ledger, monitoring, dispatch proxy or duplicate-Cursor-launch prevention is added.\n\nJira owns requirements, workstream plans and workflow status. One canonical customer configuration owns integration/deployment intent. Pigboard owns admission attempts, unresolved outcomes and reconciliation records in a customer-account DynamoDB table. Customer secrets stay in their secret store. Future worker observations, if approved, must distinguish provider truth from stored observations.\n\nManager logic uses a cloud-independent state contract with explicit conditional-update and read-consistency guarantees; AWS types and resource identifiers stay in the adapter/deployment package. Future state features should extend that coherent persistence approach, not create competing stores or configuration.\n\nDefine record versions, compatible upgrades, migration/rollback safety, retained state on removal, human reconciliation and failure tests during discovery. No unresolved admission may expire or disappear during an upgrade. A database write and Jira transition are not a single transaction; uncertainty still holds the stream for a human.\n\nContinue discovery and independent review toward the complete fixed implementation Story set for owner review. This approval does not authorize implementation, AWS provisioning or activation.",
