            "description": "Problem to solve\n\nThe accepted manager scope needs a delivery plan that produces usable increments without adding monitoring or dispatch infrastructure. Implementation Stories cannot be ready until admission contracts and deployment safety are settled.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n* Start detailed implementation grooming after PGB-67 and PGB-68 are resolved and their combined design passes two independent reviews.\n* Map the entire first release into dependency-ordered usable increments, including ticket authoring, preview, customer-owned Lambda deployment, admission and queued-repair handoffs.\n* Each Story delivers one complete outcome in one Pigboard PR, changes at most 20 files total and takes less than two human working days including testing, documentation and PR preparation.\n* Put every acceptance case on its Story with stable IDs, requirement mapping, exact approved QA skills, prerequisites, ordered actions, expected outcomes, evidence and cleanup.\n* Preserve PGB-58 delivery acceptance as a live-activation prerequisite, not a blocker to fixture-based development.\n* Review the delivery plan independently; keep uncertain future slices provisional and do not add scope or start implementation.\n\nTechnical Acceptance Criteria\n\n* One repository: https://github.com/danielchappell/Pigboard, main; use the current skills/scripts product and no archived runtime.\n* Keep estimates, dependencies, review results and release gates in one plan under the parent Epic.\n* Separate role entry points, job skills and role-specific Jira handoffs; only the manager admits In Progress.\n* Existing Jira-to-Cursor webhooks remain; monitoring, duplicate-run prevention, launch proxy, SaaS and other clouds are follow-on work.\n* Every Pigboard PR requires current-revision Codex bot thumbs-up, resolved findings, existing independent review gates and CI before merge.\n\nTesting Strategy\n\n* Audit every accepted design rule against at least one Story and acceptance case; report examined/total coverage.\n* Check every slice delivers something a customer/operator/worker can actually use and can meet all file/time/repository limits.\n* Walk install, preview, normal admission, repair order, capacity, paused Epic, failures and rollback across the proposed delivery sequence.\n* No AWS resources, delivery runs or automation activations occur in this Task.\n\nOwner review point — 14 November 2026: do not stop at discovery-task grooming. Resolve discovery, then present the complete fixed implementation Story set that delivers the Epic, with scope-to-Story coverage, dependency order, estimates, tests and independent review evidence. Owner reviews that complete set before implementation begins. Include usable AWS infrastructure-as-code installation and explicitly separate cloud-independent manager logic from the Lambda wrapper and AWS deployment package. A later cloud wrapper must not require unwinding AWS from manager rules. Changes to the approved Story set require explicit scope review.",
